// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row index_place_through_refmut_never_drops_old (tier 1).
// `q[i] = v` WHERE `q` IS `&mut [D; N]` OVERWRITES A LIVE ELEMENT WITHOUT
// RUNNING ITS DESTRUCTOR — A LEAK, silent, at both the parameter and the local
// spelling.
//
// FOUND 2026-09-07u, by the pair the index/tuple place-write repair was measured
// with, and it is NOT that repair's root: it reproduces identically at the
// PARAMETER spelling this program uses, which that repair never touches (a ref
// parameter's scope_ entry was always the caller's address). The two are
// SEPARABLE by measurement — on the base binary 337164757523a8c9 the local
// spelling read 11 (the store lost AND the old element not dropped) and this
// parameter spelling read 110 (the store lands, the old element still not
// dropped); after the repair the local spelling reads 110 too, and this program
// is unmoved.
//
// COUNT. `n` is the sum of the `v` of every D actually destructed:
//   1   the old a[0], at the store            ← MISSING, this is the defect
//   10  a[1], at scope exit
//   100 the new a[0], at scope exit
// Rust runs all three: 111. This binary prints 110 and returns 1.
//
// ROOT, by symbol, read this round: SemaChecker::lower_place_assign
// (src/compiler/sema_stmt.cpp). The liveness walk that decides `drop_old`
// bottoms out at a ROOT and its root predicate excludes Ref and Ptr — correct
// for a raw pointer (pinned by pass/placedrop_rawptr_deref_field_no_drop_old,
// which must NOT drop) and WRONG for an owning `&mut`, through which Rust drops
// the overwritten place.
//
// THE CLASS IS THE INDEX LINK, NOT THE REFERENCE ROOT — measured, two controls
// that are CORRECT on this binary and separate it:
//     let q: &mut (D, i64) = &mut t; q.0 = D{…}   -> 101, the old D dropped
//     let q: &mut S        = &mut s; q.d = D{…}   -> 101, the old D dropped
// So a TUPLE and a FIELD place through a `&mut` do drop; only the INDEX place
// does not. Any repair must keep the raw-pointer abuse direction refusing to
// drop, and must not coarsen a subscript to its container (a `*mut T` receiver
// there was the 2026-09-06n round's measured double free).
package index_place_through_refmut_never_drops_old;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
struct D { v: i64, c: *mut i64 }
impl Drop for D { fn drop(self: &mut D) { unsafe { *self.c = *self.c + self.v; } } }
fn put(q: &mut [D; 2], p: *mut i64) -> i64 { q[0i64] = D { v: 100i64, c: p }; return 0i64; }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut n: i64 = 0i64;
    let p: *mut i64 = &mut n;
    {
        let mut a: [D; 2] = [D { v: 1i64, c: p }, D { v: 10i64, c: p }];
        let k: i64 = put(&mut a, p);
        if k != 0i64 { return 3i32; }
    }
    let got: i64 = unsafe { n };
    unsafe { printf("n=%ld\n".as_ptr(), got); }
    if got != 111i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
